{0 Security notes}

This page gives the facts of version 1 that concern security. Each fact names
the tests that show it. DESIGN.md ("Authentication", "Session" and
"Verification of claims") has the details.

{1 TLS}

- The [ssl-mode] of the URL has the names of MySQL: [DISABLED], [REQUIRED],
  [VERIFY_CA] and [VERIFY_IDENTITY]. [VERIFY_CA] and [VERIFY_IDENTITY] need
  [ssl-ca] or [ssl-fingerprint=sha256:...] (a pinned certificate).
  Tests: [test/unit/test_config.ml], [test/unit/test_tls_setup.ml].
- [REQUIRED] with no pin encrypts, but it does not check the server. It gives
  no protection against an attacker in the middle. Use [VERIFY_IDENTITY], or
  a pin.
- [DISABLED] over TCP sends the two scrambles of the authentication as clear
  text. A passive listener can then attack the password offline. Use TLS over
  TCP, or a Unix socket.
- Version 1 has no TLS on a Unix socket.
- TLS needs a random generator. Call [Mirage_crypto_rng_unix.use_default ()]
  before {!Cistern.Db.with_db}. If not, {!Cistern.Db.with_db} raises
  [Invalid_argument] with this text, before any fiber starts.
  Tests: [test/sim/test_tls.ml], [test/integration/tls_tests.ml].

{1 The password}

- The full path of [caching_sha2_password] sends the password as clear text.
  Cistern sends it only to a server with a verified name, to a server with a
  pinned certificate, or on a Unix socket with the opt-in
  [allow-cleartext=socket]. Else the connect gives [Connect].
  Tests: [test/sim/test_connect.ml] ("sha2 full path on a socket with the
  opt-in"), [test/sim/test_tls.ml], [test/integration/integration.ml] ("the
  full path, the gate and the fast path").
- {!Cistern.Config.t} is abstract, so that no generic printer shows the
  password. {!Cistern.Config.pp} hides it. No error message of
  {!Cistern.Config.of_url} has a part of the URL. No value can have a control
  character.
  Tests: [test/unit/test_config.ml] ("pp hides the password", and the rows of
  [of_url]).
- The events of {!Cistern.Event} give the address of a connect, never the
  password.

{1 The values of rows}

- The SQL of an event is redacted: it has [?] in place of each value.
  {!Cistern.Error.redact} removes the message of a server error, because the
  message can hold a value of a row. The events hold redacted errors.
  Tests: [test/unit/test_raw.ml] ("redacted"), [test/unit/test_error.ml],
  [test/expect/prop_redacted.ml], [test/prop/prop_raw.ml].
- {!Cistern.Error.pp} and {!Cistern.Error.to_string} print the message of the
  server. Do not send their text to a log if the values are confidential. Use
  {!Cistern.Error.pp_summary} for logs.

{1 Raw SQL}

- {!Cistern.Raw} makes a statement from fragments:
  [Raw.sql "..." ++ Raw.v value]. A value is a literal of the client (a string
  and bytes go as hex). The SQL that Cistern makes puts each identifier in
  backquotes, with each backquote doubled. [Raw] has no function for
  identifiers: do not put a name or a value from a user in the text of
  [Raw.sql].
  Tests: [test/unit/test_literal.ml], [test/unit/test_raw.ml],
  [test/prop/prop_raw.ml].
- A raw [LOCK TABLES] or [GET_LOCK] stays on the connection until the next
  checkout resets the session.

{1 The cancel with KILL}

- Cistern sends [KILL CONNECTION] only after an exact check of the identity
  of the server, and only for a session of the same user. A user with a
  direct grant of [SUPER] (or the admin grant), or a session with an enabled
  role, turns [KILL] off ([Kill_off]). Thus a pool never kills a session of
  another user. The {{!page-index.operations}operations section} has the
  rules.
  Tests: [test/sim/test_identity.ml], [test/sim/test_kill.ml],
  [test/integration/kill_tests.ml], claims C32 and C33 of DESIGN.md.

{1 The test servers}

The test servers of the repository ([make db-up]) have a root with no
password on the Unix socket, and a fixed password for the test user. They are
only for the tests.
